# THE SALT RELIQUARY
#
# An authoring MEASUREMENT, not a demo: a dungeon written to find out what a `.dungeon`
# author can express without touching Rust. Every construct below either compiles to a real
# executor tooth or is named in the REMAINING WALLS block at the bottom.
#
# The shape: a drowned reliquary with two ways down and a warden who wants to know whose
# side you are on before he wants your money. You swear to the LEDGER and Ferrun sells you
# the gate; you swear to the DROWNED and he never deals with you again, but the long stair
# is not watched. The reliquary door asks a question you have to have LISTENED to answer,
# and Ferrun will only answer it once.

name: The Salt Reliquary
start: strand
objective: reach reliquary holding drowned_crown

room strand "The Bleaching Strand"
  Salt crust to the horizon, and a stair of black basalt going down into the water that is
  not there any more. A tithe-box, long since forced, spills tarnished coin into the crust.
  items: salt_tithe, tide_lamp
  exit down -> tidegate

room tidegate "The Tide-Gate"
  A drowned arch, the water pulled back off it, weed hanging in dead ropes. Ferrun the
  tide-warden sits in the arch's throat with a ledger and no particular hurry.
  exit up -> strand
  # THE FORK, made of teeth: the paid gate wants Ferrun's toll, and the toll is only for
  # sale to someone who swore to his ledger. The stair is only walked by the other side.
  exit east -> undervault requires flag toll_paid
  exit west -> longstair requires flag oath_allegiance_drowned

room longstair "The Long Stair"
  Three hundred steps of wet basalt with nothing on them. It is the honest way and it is
  the slow way, and the warden has never once looked down it.
  exit east -> tidegate
  exit down -> undervault

room undervault "The Undervault"
  A hall of salt-rimed niches, each one a name worn smooth. One niche is open and empty,
  and something has been dragged out of it recently.
  items: rimed_censer
  exit up -> longstair
  exit west -> tidegate
  exit north -> antechapel

room antechapel "The Antechapel"
  A low room before the reliquary door. The door is a slab of green bronze with a
  question cut into it in a script nobody living reads, and no handle at all.
  exit south -> undervault
  # The door opens for someone who knows the answer — learned by TALKING, not by looting.
  exit north -> reliquary requires flag door_answered

room reliquary "The Salt Reliquary"
  A drowned chapel with the tide still held out of it by something. On a bier of black
  salt lies the drowned crown, and it is much smaller than the stories.
  items: drowned_crown
  exit south -> antechapel

# THE OATH. Sworn once, in the arch, and it decides the rest of the dungeon. There is no
# directive that unswears it and no second branch after the first — the counter is spent.
oath allegiance in tidegate "Ferrun turns the ledger around. 'Before coin: whose side.'"
  branch ledger "You put your hand on the ledger. Ferrun almost smiles, and writes."
  branch drowned "You look past him at the niches. He closes the ledger, and does not look at you again."

# Ferrun REMEMBERS, and Ferrun CLOSES. The toll is only sold to the ledger's side; the
# name in the niche is a revelation he makes exactly once, to someone who asked properly.
npc ferrun "Ferrun, Tide-Warden" in tidegate
  about "a warden with a ledger, who has been here longer than the water has been gone"
  topic toll once -> opens toll_asked "'The toll is a tithe of salt. Not for me — for the one in the niche.'"
  topic pay once requires item salt_tithe and flag oath_allegiance_ledger -> opens toll_paid "Ferrun takes the tarnished coin, writes a line, and stands out of the arch." else "'A tithe of salt, and a hand on the ledger. I am not particular but I am not free.'"
  topic niche once requires flag toll_asked and flag oath_allegiance_ledger -> opens door_answered "'The empty niche was Aveth. The door asks who is missing. That is the answer.'" else "'Ask me a proper question, and be someone I answer.'"
  topic crown -> reveals "'It is smaller than the stories. Everything down there is.'"

# The censer against the bronze: one swing, and the salt smoke is spent.
use rimed_censer once on door in antechapel -> flag censer_swung "Salt smoke curls against the bronze. The question does not care, and the censer is cold."


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# REMAINING WALLS — things I wanted writing this file and still cannot say.
# (W2 forks, W5 conjunctions and W6 once-only were walls when this file was first
# written; they are the constructs used above. These four are what is left.)
#
#  W1. NO COST. `salt_tithe` is still in hand after Ferrun takes it. `has_<item>` is a
#      monotone boolean and nothing in the grammar lowers it. Tolls, offerings,
#      ammunition, rations — every economy is unwritable. `once` gets me "you may pay
#      only one toll"; it does not get me "and now the coin is gone".
#
#  W3. ONE ENDING. `objective:` is a single `reach ROOM holding ITEM`. The oath forks the
#      MIDDLE of the dungeon and both branches still end the same way — the drowned's
#      ending, where you leave the crown on the bier, is not representable.
#
#  W4. NO RISK. `lose:`, `hostile`, `combat`, `light`, `spell`, `consumable`, `status` and
#      `player_hp:` all parse and are then refused BY NAME at compile. A deployed authored
#      dungeon is a puzzle box you cannot die in. The long stair is supposed to cost you
#      lamp-oil; the `light` block that says so does not deploy, so `tide_lamp` is scenery.
#
#  W7. NO CHECK. Nothing rolls. `skills.rs` has d20-vs-DC on the real substrate; the
#      grammar cannot name an ability, a DC, or an outcome that differs on success.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
